Subject: Report a class's call operators through `ParseCallbacks`

A member C++ calls `operator()` reaches `Function::parse` and is dropped there.
With `--represent-cxx-operators` the name is checked against the operators
bindgen has a Rust spelling for, `operator()` is not among them, and the parse
returns `ParseError::Continue`; without that option the name is refused sooner,
for not being an identifier. Either way `CompInfo::from_ty` sees a member it got
no function for and moves on, so nothing bindgen generates says that a class is
callable at all.

`--report-call-operators` keeps what C++ said about each of them and reports it
through `denote_call_operator`: how many parameters it declares, the constness,
and the access. Nothing is generated with the option on either. Naming one is
the hard half - `operator()` is no identifier, and a name invented for it has to
stay clear of the class's own members - and that is a question this leaves open;
what it closes is the class which looks, in the output, exactly like a class
which declares no such thing.

The report is made where the member was dropped: the arm which takes the
cursors `Item::parse` returned nothing for. A member reported from there is one
bindgen really did decline, so an option which later generated call operators
would stop these being reported without a second decision having to be kept in
step with the first.

Which cursor that is has to be read from the cursor itself rather than from the
name `Function::parse` worked with. `ParseCallbacks::generated_name_override`
runs before that name is examined, so a consumer which renames every function -
and one which renames every function is the consumer most likely to want this -
leaves nothing there to recognise. `clang_getCursorSpelling` is unaffected by
the rename and answers `operator()`.

No signature is reported, only the count of parameters. That is what tells one
overload of `operator()` from another, which is as much as a consumer needs to
say how many of them a class has; a signature would be the first half of
generating them, which this does not do. The count is of what C++ declared
rather than of what a call must pass, a parameter being free to have a default
argument.

A call operator template is not reported here. libclang gives one a
`CXCursor_FunctionTemplate`, which `CompInfo::from_ty` never parses as a member,
and `--report-member-function-templates` already speaks for those.

The report itself is made from `CompInfo::codegen`, beside the reports every
class makes, and is subject to `--generate=methods` as any other member
function is. There is no per-member blocklist to consult, a call operator never
becoming an item for a blocklist to match, and a blocklisted class does not
reach codegen at all.

Opt-in, because a report made unconditionally would be a new one for every
consumer which already implements `ParseCallbacks`. With the option off nothing
changes at all.

